WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:04.230
The sort method sorts a list.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:11.760
I will change the reverse statement to a sort,
rerun the code, and we will see that the list

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.639
is now in alphabetical order.

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:20.420
Before we go any further, I want to cover
another basic programming concept with you.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:26.710
You may notice that every method we have used
so far has parentheses.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:31.560
Basically, every method in every programming
language has parentheses because methods can

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.989
contain arguments, meaning settings, or items
that are being manipulated.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:37.730
Take this sort method.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:44.300
There are no arguments yet, so we have the
default sort, which is in ascending order.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:51.460
However, I can add an argument, reverse=True,
inside the parentheses.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.969
Remember, Boolean values start with a capital
letter.

00:00:55.969 --> 00:01:05.269
I will rerun the code, and we see the list
of cities in reverse order.

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:11.490
One aspect of this code we have yet to work
with is the list of populations on line 5.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:17.869
These populations coincide with these cities.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:22.210
We want to know the largest population among
these cities and the smallest population among

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.180
these cities.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.250
The max and min functions identify those values,
respectively.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:38.040
I will add a print statement on line 10 to
print the max of the population variable.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:44.009
I will run the code, and we see that among
the populations, 1.6 million is the largest

00:01:44.009 --> 00:01:48.480
value, again representing the max function.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:54.490
I will change the max to min, rerun the code,
and we will see the smallest population of

00:01:54.490 --> 00:02:00.870
32000, again referencing the populations variable
and the min function.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:04.450
The last list operation we will cover is the
length of a list.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:08.300
As people collect items in games, there may
come a time when we need to display how many

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.180
items they have collected.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.250
The len method displays the length of a list.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:21.290
I will add a print statement on line 11 to
print the length of the capitals list.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:29.290
The code is print(len(capitals)).

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:37.860
In rerunning the code, the length is three,
as we have three capital cities in the list.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.190
We have reached the end of data type operations,
so let’s do a little analysis exercise for

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:43.900
a code block.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:51.330
I will open the 126-analyze file, and we have
a quote and a type of list.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:56.620
Use the second part of the Review 1.2 exercise
in the project workbook to determine what

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:03.290
this print statement will return, and if this
statement will work, and then we will review

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:04.879
this code and reveal the answers.

